document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[18]}],"walking":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[19]}],"from":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[19]}],"root":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[19]}],"deepest":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[19]}],"matching":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[20]}],"costs":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[20]}],"proportional":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[20]}],"length":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[20]}],"small":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[20]}],"then":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[21]}],"collects":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[21]}],"completion":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[21]}],"add":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[23]}],"relevance":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[23]}],"feedback":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[23]}],"spelling":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[23]}],"correction":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[23]}],"caching":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[23]}],"remains":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[24]}],"heart":[{"document":"search_engines.txt","count":1,"lines":[24]}]},"documents":{"algorithms.txt":["Algorithms and Data Structures","","An algorithm is a finite sequence of well-defined instructions for solving a problem.","Every algorithm must be correct, terminate, and use resources efficiently.","","Data structures organize information so algorithms can operate efficiently.","The array is the simplest data structure: constant-time access by index.","The linked list trades random access for cheap insertion and removal.","A hash table maps keys to buckets and gives average constant-time lookup.","The hash table is the backbone of every modern inverted index.","","Sorting is a classical family of algorithms.","Bubble sort is simple but slow for large inputs.","Merge sort divides the array, sorts the halves, and merges them in linear time.","Quicksort partitions the array around a pivot and is fast in practice.","Heap sort builds a binary heap and repeatedly extracts the maximum.","","Graphs model relationships between objects.","Breadth-first search explores a graph level by level using a queue.","Depth-first search dives deep along one path using a stack or recursion.","Dijkstra's algorithm finds shortest paths when edge weights are non-negative.","","Big O notation describes how running time grows with input size.","An O(1) hash table lookup beats an O(n) linear scan for large collections."],"data_structures.txt":["Data Structures Reference","","Dictionary and Hash Table","A dictionary stores key-value pairs. A hash table implements the dictionary","by hashing each key into a bucket index. Collisions are resolved by chaining","or by open addressing. Average lookup, insertion, and deletion take O(1).","","Linked List","A linked list is a chain of nodes where each node points to the next node.","Insertion at the head is O(1); searching for a value is O(n).","","Stack and Queue","The stack is last-in first-out and supports push and pop.","The queue is first-in first-out and supports enqueue and dequeue.","Graph traversal uses a queue for breadth-first search and a stack or","recursion for depth-first search.","","Tree","A binary search tree keeps sorted keys: smaller keys go left, larger keys","go right. Balanced trees such as AVL and red-black trees guarantee O(log n)","search, insertion, and deletion. The trie, or prefix tree, is a special tree","that stores strings character by character and powers autocomplete.","","Heap","A binary heap is a complete tree stored inside an array. The min-heap","serves the smallest element in O(log n) and backs priority queues.","","Graph","A graph is a set of vertices connected by edges. Representations include","the adjacency matrix and the adjacency list. Shortest paths, cycles, and","connectivity are classical graph problems."],"history_of_computing.txt":["The History of Computing","","Charles Babbage designed the Analytical Engine in the nineteenth century.","Ada Lovelace wrote what is considered the first algorithm intended for a machine.","","Alan Turing formalized computation with the Turing machine in 1936.","The Turing machine is a mathematical model that defines what is computable.","","The ENIAC, completed in 1945, was among the first electronic general-purpose","computers. It filled a room and consumed enormous power.","","Transistors replaced vacuum tubes, and integrated circuits packed thousands","of components onto a single chip. Moore observed that transistor density","doubled roughly every two years.","","The personal computer arrived in the late seventies. Graphical interfaces,","the mouse, and the spreadsheet transformed computing into a mass product.","","The World Wide Web, proposed by Tim Berners-Lee in 1989, connected documents","across the globe with hyperlinks. Search engines such as AltaVista and later","Google made the web navigable, and the inverted index became the most","important data structure of the information age."],"search_engines.txt":["Information Retrieval and Search Engines","","A search engine answers user queries by consulting an index built in advance.","The core structure is the inverted index: a hash table that maps each word","to the list of documents containing that word.","","Indexing proceeds in three phases.","First, the crawler scans the folder and reads every text file.","Second, the tokenizer sanitizes the text: punctuation is stripped, letters","are lowercased, and common stopwords such as the, is, of, and to are removed.","Third, the indexer writes postings: for each word, one entry per document","recording the occurrence count and the line numbers of every match.","","At query time the engine looks the word up in the inverted index in","constant time, ranks documents by term frequency, and extracts a short","snippet around the first match so the user can preview the context.","","A trie, or prefix tree, stores the whole vocabulary and answers prefix","queries for autocomplete. Walking the trie from the root to the deepest","matching node costs time proportional to the prefix length, and a small","depth-first traversal then collects every stored completion.","","Modern engines add relevance feedback, spelling correction, and caching,","but the inverted index remains the data structure at the heart of search."]}}